# DockerLabs work queue — one line per lab, consumed by download-labs.sh
# Format:  <platform_id>|<label>|<difficulty>|<why it is in the queue>
#
# `why` records the selection criterion, so a later session can tell whether a
# lab is still the right pick or was added on a hunch. The pilot's rule was:
# cover a class the methodology does not have, not a class that is merely easy.
#
# --- Batch 1: pilot, 5/5 solved -------------------------------------------------
# Chosen to span stacks the first five did not (WordPress, CI/CD, monitoring,
# video platform) and to feed untouched repo sections.
148|Spain|dificil|buffer overflow + Python pickle deserialization; both absent from the repo
292|Acme|muy-facil|pre-auth credential channel, world-writable docroot, execution identity
281|PipePwned|medio|self-hosted CI runner abuse; runner identity vs writer identity
293|Zabbixploit|medio|SQLi, session hijack, monitoring plane; target vector blocked by a working allowlist
271|BaluHome|medio|stored XSS -> cookie without httpOnly -> RCE; multiple tester self-corrections
# --- Batch 2: cover what batch 1 did not ---------------------------------------
295|Baremetal|medio|IPMI/BMC design error -> hash -> GRUB bypass; bare metal and BMC absent
62|Raas|dificul|SMB enumeration + reversing a ransomware binary (AES-256-CBC) with Ghidra
209|Profetas|medio|XXE + Python deobfuscation + sudoers; XXE absent from the repo
146|Elevator|dificul|Windows privilege escalation; the first five had no Windows target
255|PinguPenguin|dificul|Spring Boot actuator; framework-specific web class absent
186|Tokenaso|dificul|race condition + credential exposure + crypto; no concurrency oracle exists
26|404-not-found|facil|described as LDAP injection; the code was never executed and the process could not speak LDAP
# --- Batch 3: the gaps the audit named, in labs that actually teach them ------
# The LDAP/Active Directory gap in active_directory.md is real and CANNOT be closed
# here: no lab on this platform ships an actual directory. It needs a target with
# one, and writing that section from a lab without LDAP would be unmeasured advice.
# These five are the gaps that do have a lab.
113|Road_To_Olympus|dificul|pivoting through three hosts with Chisel; pivoting and tunnelling have no oracle
65|Seeker|medio|subdomain fuzzing over virtual hosting; the confirming case for the wildcard rule from 404-not-found
6|Grandma|dificul|LFI in aiohttp (a real CVE) plus tunnelling; CVE documentation and multi-hop transport
23|reverse|medio|log poisoning to RCE through LFI; log poisoning has no oracle anywhere
33|chmod-4755|medio|rbash escape and SUID after SMB enumeration; restricted-shell escape is a distinct class
# --- Batch 4: the classes still with no oracle anywhere in the repo -------------
141|DockerLabs|facil|container security: socket exposure, privileged mode, capabilities, namespace escape
238|Autoescuela|facil|Node/Express WebSocket to RCE under a named CVE; WebSockets have no oracle
163|Ofuskeit|medio|JavaScript deobfuscation and API interaction; reading client code as attack surface
118|los40ladrones|facil|port knocking plus Hydra credential attack; no credential-attack doctrine exists
129|DockHackLab|medio|PHP upload and SSH key injection to escalate; authorized_keys injection is a distinct class
# --- Batch 5: classes still absent, chosen because the platform has no lab for
# GraphQL, request smuggling, CORS, NoSQL or OAuth. These are the gaps it *can* fill.
90|Norc|dificul|CVE-2023-6063, SQLi in a WordPress plugin; a real advisory with disclosure history
283|Los 3 Hackers|facil|evasion techniques and a SQLi blacklist; evasion and denylist filtering have no oracle
93|Pinguinazo|facil|SSTI then an interpreter granted through sudo; a sudo grant naming an interpreter is its own class
268|kmspwned|facil|credential reuse then escalation through a cron job; cron privesc has no oracle
36|Verdejo|facil|XSS, SSTI and hash cracking; the repo ships four wordlists and no doctrine for breaking
85|Aidor|facil|IDOR with a session rewritten from a request parameter; IDOR is not unauthenticated access, and a curl cookie jar that only reads is a false negative
172|Crossfi|medio|CSRF at two levels; a CSRF token stored in the session cookie is forgeable, and the Referer check ran after the write
296|Asturias|medio|unprotected endpoints plus a vulnerability the class is absent from the repo
# --- Batch 6: the first mobile/APK lab. The platform names no attack surface, no
# mechanism and no control, so the class had to be defined from scratch.
264|ApkAdmin|facil|Android exported Activity with no permission whose only gate is a boolean extra the caller supplies; mobile/APK is an entire absent domain, not merely an uncovered one
249|Adopting|medio|web cache deception, the class with no evidence anywhere in the repo; the cache key omits identity entirely and the classic /x.pdf/foo.css route-confusion form is REFUTED here - the real leak is a legitimate dynamic route whose extension is on the cacheable list, and Cache-Control: private, no-store is a request, not a control
117|Pressenter|facil|WordPress platform: one lab exists (90 Norc) and a platform is not a class
108|Whoiam|facil|WordPress platform: enumeration and role model, absent as a class
102|Escolares|facil|WordPress platform; plugin surface not yet catalogued
87|WalkingCMS|facil|WordPress platform; content and author enumeration
220|CuentaAtras|medio|WordPress platform; account lifecycle
61|BadPlugin|medio|WordPress platform; the plugin is the surface, and plugin trust is its own class
115|Master|medio|WordPress platform; admin-reach chain
12|Collections|medio|WordPress platform; content-type handling
112|Asucar|medio|WordPress platform; user enumeration to auth
32|Vulnerame|dificil|WordPress platform; a harder instance to test whether the rule generalises
218|Internal|facil|command injection with WAF bypass plus local brute force; a concrete WAF is absent
98|Redirection|facil|open redirect; never treated and it is its own token class
169|SpiderRoot|medio|WAF bypass plus port forwarding plus Linux privesc
84|Waffy|dificil|ModSecurity bypass under Apache; evasion exists but no WAF instance does
73|ChocoPing|medio|WAF bypass class
189|Rolarola|medio|command injection; deserialisation coverage is pickle-only so far
219|Talent|medio|WordPress platform plus Linux privesc
282|hannah_coffee|facil|web app on Linux; queue picked by difficulty since the class-gap scan is exhausted
243|Duque|facil|two web vulnerabilities; tests whether a second finding gets filed separately
188|Wargames|facil|recon and service analysis; enumeration with control pairs, the class the corpus is thinnest on
168|PingCTF|facil|command execution; a covered class, kept as a generalisation test
167|WinFake|facil|source-code inspection as the entry point; the only lab where reading the code IS the first step
82|E jotapete|facil|Drupal 8.5.0: the unit of trust is the compiled service container, not a list; third platform in the corpus
83|Grooti|facil|enumeration as the deliverable; the decisive finding is on no service, and the advertised database leg cannot be entered
254|Gotham|facil|**NO OBTENIBLE, truncado y el servidor miente sobre Content-Length**: sirve 107,479,040 bytes = 102,5 MiB exactos (limite redondo de proxy) y reporta ESA longitud como completa; las propias entradas del archivo declaran 107,575,879, asi que faltan 96.839 bytes. El `gotham.tar` de dentro diria 337.918.976 sin comprimir
166|InfluencerHate|facil|brute force on a web form, then a second escalation route
162|PkgPoison|facil|simple intrusion then privesc by a distinct route
78|Bypassme|facil|information disclosure, web fuzzing, credential leakage
77|galeria|facil|simple web fuzzing and a file upload vulnerability
245|TrailPack|medio|several web vulnerabilities in one lab
242|WorkConnect|medio|enumerating platform DNS records
234|SECorNOTsec|medio|exploiting a command injection
